  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Federal contract obligations to Louisiana total $3.6B for FY2026 to date. Award activity shows 19,439 awards with an average award size of $188K. Obligations fell 23.2% year over year. That drop comes after a peak of $4.8B in FY25 and follows year-to-year swings: FY21 $4B, FY22 $3.7B, FY23 $4.5B, FY24 $3.9B, FY25 $4.8B, FY26 $3.6B. The largest single buying agencies so far are Department of Homeland Security with $1.5B and Department of Defense with $1.4B. Ship Building and Repairing is the largest industry by obligations at $1.6B. Major vendors include BOLLINGER SHIPYARDS LOCKPORT, L.L.C. at $1.3B and STRATEGIC STORAGE PARTNERS, LLC. at $424M. This mix shows concentration in shipbuilding and defense-related buying, with a small number of large vendors and agencies accounting for a big share of the total obligations.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How does FY2026 to date compare with prior fiscal years?",
        "body": "FY2026 to date obligations are $3.6B. That is lower than FY25 $4.8B and FY23 $4.5B, and it reflects that obligations fell 23.2% year over year. The historical series is FY21 $4B, FY22 $3.7B, FY23 $4.5B, FY24 $3.9B, FY25 $4.8B, FY26 $3.6B, showing swings rather than steady growth."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies and vendors drive most of the spending in Louisiana?",
        "body": "Department of Homeland Security and Department of Defense together account for $1.5B and $1.4B in obligations respectively. On the vendor side, BOLLINGER SHIPYARDS LOCKPORT, L.L.C. has $1.3B in obligations and STRATEGIC STORAGE PARTNERS, LLC. has $424M. The largest industry by obligations is Ship Building and Repairing at $1.6B, which aligns with the top vendors and top agencies' spending patterns."
      }
    ]
  },
